3.04  STRUCTURAL BREAK SCAN (AUTO-DETECTED)
================================================================================

  Scanned 29 candidate break points across 41 months

Top 3 Break Candidates by Chow F-statistic
------------------------------------------
  - Chow test for a structural break in the aggregate series: the null is that the same relationship holds before and after the candidate date (Chow, 1960).
  - A low p-value indicates the series mean/variance shifts at that point. That matters for the split, because a break inside the test window means the test period is not drawn from the training regime.
  - Reported as the three strongest candidates rather than one verdict: with a single series and many candidate dates this is exploratory, and the multiple-comparisons problem is real.

   date  chow_f  chow_p  mean_ratio  std_ratio
2025-12   2.676  0.1099       1.358      1.276
2025-05   1.663  0.2048       1.239      1.109
2026-01   1.547  0.2210       1.285      1.325

  No significant structural break (best candidate p=0.1099 >= 0.05)
  -> A pooled model across the full range is supportable.

================================================================================
3.05  STATIONARITY TESTING -- PER-BRAND ADF
================================================================================

  H0: non-stationary  |  p < 0.05 -> stationary
  Testing raw, log1p, and first-difference


ADF Test per Brand (top brands by volume)
-----------------------------------------
  - Augmented Dickey-Fuller test per brand. Null hypothesis is a unit root, i.e. non-stationarity; p < 0.05 rejects it and indicates a stationary series (Dickey & Fuller, 1979).
  - Failing to reject is weak evidence -- ADF has low power on short series, and these brands have at most ~46 monthly observations. Read a non-rejection as inconclusive, not as proof of a unit root.
  - Non-stationary brands are the argument for differencing or for lag features that let the model absorb the trend rather than extrapolate it.

