================================================================================
3.07  ZERO-SALES CHARACTERISATION
================================================================================

Zero-Sales Characterisation across 101 brands
---------------------------------------------
  - Brands classified by where zero-sales months occur: leading zeros (not yet launched), trailing (delisted), intermittent (genuinely sporadic demand), or none.
  - The distinction drives treatment. Leading and trailing zeros are structural absence and should be trimmed; intermittent zeros are real demand behaviour and must be kept, since removing them would bias the target upward.
  - Intermittent demand also breaks the usual accuracy metrics -- MAPE is undefined at zero, which constrains metric choice downstream.

    type  brands
no zeros     101

  Brands with any zeros: 0 / 101

  Imputation implications:
    Clustered (entry/exit) -> exclude pre-entry periods; carry a distribution indicator
    Scattered (data gaps)  -> forward-fill up to 2 periods; flag with a missingness indicator
    Rolling means must handle zeros before computing, or lags understate the true trend.

